    "q-124008": {
      "dom_id": "step-q124008",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Let $f(x,y,z)={e}^{x}+{e}^{y}+{e}^{z}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨t,{t}^{2},2{t}^{3}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0,0⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,1,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,1,2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,1,2)-f(0,0,0) \\\\  & =({e}^{1}+{e}^{1}+{e}^{2})-({e}^{0}+{e}^{0}+{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =(2e+{e}^{2})-3 \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+2e-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
