    "q-104626": {
      "dom_id": "step-q104626",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Let $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}).$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ for $x\\in [-1,2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,2{t}^{2}⟩,\\,t\\in [-1,2].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨-1,2⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨2,8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(-1,2)$ and $(2,8).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(-1)) \\\\  & =f(2,8)-f(-1,2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({2}^{3}+{8}^{3})-\\frac{1}{3}((-1)^{3}+{2}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(520)-\\frac{1}{3}(7) \\\\  & =\\frac{520}{3}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =171.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-104626-a",
